                <h2>Why Accounts Can't Be Managed Uniformly and Need Layering</h2>
<p>Many teams start with just two or three accounts and see no need for layering. But when accounts exceed 10 or 20, problems emerge:</p>
<p><strong>Unified budgets and strategies mask issues</strong>. When one account spends $500 per day and another spends $50, managing them with the same standards causes you to miss optimization opportunities.</p>
<p>The core logic of layered management is: <strong>resources are always limited, prioritize high-value accounts</strong>. Focus energy and budget on validated accounts while leaving sufficient testing space for new ones.</p>
<hr />
<h2>What Are Common Dimensions for Account Layering?</h2>
<p>There's no standard answer for layering, but three dimensions are most commonly used:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>ROAS/Conversion Performance</strong>: Whether targets are consistently met is the most direct judgment criterion.</li>
<li><strong>Spend Scale</strong>: Daily and monthly spend determine account weight.</li>
<li><strong>Stability</strong>: Whether performance has been steady over the past 30 days or highly volatile.</li>
</ul>
<p>A common three-layer structure:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Core Accounts</strong>: ROAS meets targets, spending is stable,投放history exceeds 60 days.</li>
<li><strong>Testing Accounts</strong>: Still running素材and audience tests, not yet stable in conversions.</li>
<li><strong>New Accounts</strong>: Just opened, still verifying feasibility.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>How to Allocate Budget Across Different Layers?</h2>
<p>Budget allocation should follow layering, not gut feeling.</p>
<p><strong>Core accounts</strong> take 70%+ of total budget, with the goal of stable output without frequent adjustments.</p>
<p><strong>Testing accounts</strong> occupy about 20%, aiming to validate new creatives and audiences, with enough space to run data.</p>
<p><strong>New accounts</strong> use 5-10% of budget for quick testing, judging within 3-7 days whether they're worth upgrading.</p>
<p>This ratio is a reference—adjust based on team size and business stage. E-commerce teams chasing viral products may need higher testing ratios; brand-focused teams may allocate more to core layers.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How to Avoid Internal Competition Between Accounts?</h2>
<p>Internal competition manifests in two areas: <strong>audience overlap</strong> and <strong>budget competition</strong>.</p>
<p>Audience overlap is most common. The same users are repeatedly reached across different accounts, ad frequency increases, and conversion costs rise. Solutions:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Audience Segmentation</strong>: Different accounts correspond to different audience packages by region, age group, or interest tags.</li>
<li><strong>Frequency Caps</strong>: Set ad frequency limits to avoid overexposure to the same users.</li>
</ul>
<p>Budget competition occurs when multiple accounts compete for the same traffic, driving up bidding but not improving overall spending efficiency. The solution is establishing a <strong>unified budget pool</strong>, dynamically allocating based on layering and real-time performance rather than each account acting independently.</p>
<hr />
<h2>How to Design Team Permissions After Layering?</h2>
<p>Layering isn't just about accounts—it's about team collaboration too.</p>
<p><strong>Core accounts</strong> go to the most experienced advertisers, with tighter operational permissions—budget adjustments require supervisor approval.</p>
<p><strong>Testing accounts</strong> can be managed by mid-level advertisers, with reasonable room for trial and error, but weekly data reviews are required.</p>
<p><strong>New accounts</strong> can be rotated, allowing team members to quickly familiarize themselves with new account setup while observing who has higher testing efficiency.</p>
<p>Permission design should be paired with an <strong>account health dashboard</strong>: ROAS, spend, and anomaly alerts visible at a glance, so managers can quickly identify problem accounts rather than checking each one individually.</p>
<hr />
<h2>When Account Performance Declines, How to Decide Downgrade or Closure?</h2>
<p>This is where teams most often stumble. When account ROAS drops below 50% of target for 3 consecutive days, investigate:</p>
<ul>
<li>Is the market fluctuating?</li>
<li>Is the creative fatigued?</li>
<li>Is audience targeting too narrow or too broad?</li>
</ul>
<p>First round of optimization: swap creatives, adjust audiences, lower bids. If no significant recovery within 7 days, <strong>directly downgrade to testing layer</strong>, reduce budget, and observe for two more weeks.</p>
<p>No improvement in testing layer either? Close it. Don't be sentimental—sunk costs are the most expensive costs. Redirecting budget to productive accounts is the correct choice.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Layered management isn't a one-time project</strong>—reassess layer standards quarterly. When market conditions or business goals change, layering strategy must adapt too.</p>
<p>The essence of account layering is focusing the team's limited time where it creates the most value. Get these 6 questions right, and multi-account management is no longer a tangled mess.</p>
